; A `.cbor` over a reference to an ALIAS rule, in top-level rule-BODY position
; (`x = bytes .cbor bar` with `bar = uint`), GENERATES — the sibling position to
; tests/robustness/choice_cbor_ref_arm.cddl, kept as its own row because it reaches the failure
; through a different site and needed a different fix.
;
; What it pins is that the rule-BODY registration strips the target's `Alias` wrapper before it
; registers, so `x = bytes .cbor bar` lowers to a transparent alias. Without that strip the target
; arrives as an already-`Alias`-wrapped `RustType`, and registering the rule body trips
; `register_type_alias`'s first assertion, which forbids exactly that. The strip belongs to THIS
; seam alone (`parsing::strip_alias_for_registration`): a MEMBER or type-choice arm keeps the node,
; which is what routes an annotated target's custom codec and types the field by the alias name.
; Stripping costs nothing structural here — a CDDL alias rule over a primitive generates a
; TRANSPARENT `pub type Bar = u64;`, so the alias and its target are the same Rust type — and the
; wire facts written on the target travel across the strip with it.
;
; This row is also the reason the rule-graph edge that fixes the choice-arm sibling cannot ship on
; its own. That edge orders the target BEFORE its user, which is correct; but it means the body sees
; a resolved `Alias` where it used to see an unregistered forward reference. Whether it saw one or
; the other used to depend on how the two rule NAMES happened to sort — so this shape generated or
; aborted by coincidence of naming. Both fixes together make the outcome the same for every name.
x = bytes .cbor bar
bar = uint
